    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d338">

                <pb facs="0546" n="260" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg260"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/2655</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-05-06T13:00:00+02:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 6, 1916, 1 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received May 7, 9.30 a. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3852. Looking over my 3839<anchor n="1" xml:id="d338-f1"/> I also recall that the
                    Emperor spoke of England’s stopping cargoes bound for Germany through neutral
                    countries. I said I might be wrong but I thought that during our Civil War
                    England had sent cargoes really intended for the South to Mexican ports and that
                    we had stopped these cargoes, and that England had conceded the point, making it
                    difficult for us to insist on a contrary ruling now. In addition to saying that
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Lusitania</hi> was warned, the Emperor said England
                    was really responsible, as English had made the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Lusitania</hi> go slowly in English waters so that the Germans could
                    torpedo it and so bring on trouble.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
